 at the  age of 13. there were so many interviews  with enslaved people that echoed you  know her experiences so many enslaved  women recounting  being sexually abused by their owners  and so I was able to use the  documentation of experiences of other  enslaved women to be able to weave that  into what her experiences were like so  many Mary Lumpkin survived and one year  after the Civil War ended Robert Lumpkin  died leaving everything to the woman  quote who resides with me she had  ensured that her kids were educated she  had ensured that they were freed she had  freed herself and that she had later  inherited this jail  this violent place and enabled it to  become one of America's first hbcus  that's because by 1867 she leased the  old jail to baptist Minister Dr  Nathaniel Colver who turned it into a  religious school for newly free black  people the devil's half acre then became  God's half acre and by 1899 something  more Virginia Union University just a  few miles down the road our founding  mother Mary lumpkins represents for us  what it means to experience America  today  as president of vuu Dr Hakeem Lucas  recognized Lumpkin with this Cornerstone  and named a street in her honor to allow  her Legacy to be known for providing a  jail as a place for education and  empowerment  is the story that we're constantly  shaping for our students over the last  two decades activists have launched the  restoration efforts of a burial ground  and slave Trail one that retraces the  force migration into the Deep South this  is a part of a two and a half mile Trail  Virginia State Delegate Dolores mcquinn  has been its Steward keeping Mary's gift  in perspective the first elected  African-American Governor came out there  we have Mayors we have leaders and  pastors men and women who also went  through Virginia Union Universe  including myself for green connecting  the long lost branches of the Lumpkin  family tree was a challenge because so  many had escaped racism through the  generations by denying their Birthright  all I had was descendants who didn't  know anything about her and  um  who couldn't necessarily connect to her  story because they had lived as white  but then just before finishing her book  green found Dr Cara Olivia Herron  lumpkin's great-great-granddaughter and  while she takes pride in one ancestor  she arms herself against the shame she  feels for the other I hate the fact that  I'm his descendant and I went to the  house of musical Traditions to come  apart DC Maryland and brought this to  where as a way of protesters anger I had  to put the anger somewhere and this is  why I wore this for green it comes down  to the common Humanity we all share one  that in her book she links back to Mary  Lumpkin before she was anything else  Mary Lumpkin was someone's daughter  before she was the mother of a slave  Trader's children and a woman seeking  freedom for herself and her descendants  she was an innocent baby girl  a lot to unpack in that book you know so  many contradictions how this enslaver  this cruel enslaver as so much was  written about him could hand over his  fortune to the woman who lived with him  as his concubine he must have felt  something for her but the question is  what was it that she felt for him you  know we deal with this today it was  fascinating it's fascinating to me  Michelle that you have a place that was  you know physically imprisoned people  that is a place now where you free your  mind for Education absolutely and what  that has brought to so many since yeah  it has brought quite a bit I mean  Governor Doug Wilder who then went on to  Howard University law school then became  this amazing footnote to history is is  the first black governor in the United  States elected in this country and how  she was able to go back and just unwind  all this yeah yeah and investigates  pretty fascinating yeah ancestry.com  played a bit as well well that thank you  ancestry.com thanks Michelle great story

